  "title": "print: Write an empty string for raw JSON hex with null tvb",
  "description": "json_write_field_hex_value gets called to write either the value of an\nobject, or in some cases to write a value in an array. When writing to\nan array for the JSON Raw output, each array index has a specified\nmeaning - the first index is the value, then the later indices are\nthe position, length, bitmask, and type.\n\nIf called, it *must* write something, otherwise either there will be\nan error from the json dumper getting unbalanced in the former case\n( json_dumper error: setting name twice on an object member), or\nall the subsequent array indices will be shifted over and json2pcap.py\nor other consumers will either interpret the indices incorrectly or\npossibly throw an exception because the array length is shorter than\nexpected. (At best, they will ignore the bad data.)\n\nSeveral of the other potential callers avoid this situation in most\ncases by not calling the function if the field_info has length 0, but\n-T jsonraw calls the function with zero length if the ordinary values\nare not used.\n\nWrite an empty string, which is what is currently done if the field\nhas a length but its data source tvb is not found in the list of data\nsources (because the tvb was not added with add_new_data_source.)\nIt might make sense to use JSON null instead, though.",
